&lt;p&gt;In 1974, thousands of shattered bits of limestone surfaced in a Sardinian field. Now reassembled into dozens of colossal statues, they tell of a powerful Mediterranean civilization from the Bronze Age and the Iron Age.&lt;br /&gt;
These figures and their site, a monumental necropolis, are among the most important archaeological discoveries of the past fifty years.&amp;nbsp;&lt;br /&gt;
Expert researchers will gather for a&amp;nbsp;symposium about discoveries made at this site by archaeologists, historians, conservators, and restorers.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;After the talks, we&amp;#39;ll celebrate the opening of a new gallery exhibition.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This event is in-person only.&lt;/p&gt;
